
##############################################################################################
# [1] the accelerated-worldline embedding: Z(Delta_tau) and kappa_eff=sqrt(H^2+a^2) (sympy, EXACT)
##############################################################################################

 dS_4 = hyperboloid X.X = 1/H^2 in M^{1,4}. Static-patch worldline at fixed areal radius r0 (uniformly
 accelerated, proper accel a = H^2 r0 / sqrt(1-H^2 r0^2)). The dS-invariant Z = H^2 X(tau).X(tau'):
     Z(Delta_tau) = s^2 cosh(kappa Delta_tau) + (1-s^2),   s=sqrt(1-H^2 r0^2),  kappa = H/s.
 This is PURE GEOMETRY (the field mass never enters). Derive kappa^2 = H^2 + a^2.
  s = sqrt(1-H^2 r0^2),  kappa = H/s,  a = H^2 r0/sqrt(1-H^2 r0^2)
  kappa^2 - (H^2 + a^2) = 0
   [PASS] embedding gives kappa_eff^2 = H^2 + a^2 EXACTLY (geometric, mass-independent)
  Z(Delta_tau) - 1 = H**2*r0**2 - (H**2*r0**2 - 1)*cosh(Deltatau*H/sqrt(-H**2*r0**2 + 1)) - 1  = s^2 (cosh(kappa Delta_tau) - 1)
   [PASS] Z=1 <=> cosh(kappa Delta_tau)=1 (the coincidence/KMS-image condition is geometric)
  cosh(kappa Delta_tau)=1  =>  kappa Delta_tau = 2 pi i n  =>  nearest pole Delta_tau = 2 pi i/kappa_eff
   [PASS] nearest KMS pole at Delta_tau = 2pi i/kappa_eff (period 2pi i/kappa_eff -> T=kappa_eff/2pi)

##############################################################################################
# [2] the GENERAL-MASS dS 2-point function G(Z): singular ONLY at Z=1, mass-independent LEADING
##############################################################################################

 The de Sitter-invariant Wightman two-point function for a scalar of mass m in dS_4 is (Bunch-Davies):
     G(Z) = (H^2/16 pi^2) Gamma(h_+) Gamma(h_-) 2F1(h_+, h_-; 2; (1+Z)/2),
     h_pm = 3/2 +- nu,   nu = sqrt(9/4 - m^2/H^2).
 (nu real & in (0,3/2): complementary series; nu = i mu imaginary: principal series, heavy m>3H/2;
  nu=1/2 i.e. m^2=2H^2: the conformally-coupled/massless-conformal point used in the pullback.)
 2F1(a,b;c;x) has its branch point at x=1, i.e. (1+Z)/2 = 1 -> Z=1 (coincidence). With c-a-b = 2-h_+-h_- =
 2-3 = -1 < 0, near x->1:  2F1 ~ Gamma(c)Gamma(a+b-c)/(Gamma(a)Gamma(b)) (1-x)^{c-a-b} = ...(1-x)^{-1}.
 Compute the LEADING coincidence coefficient of G and show the mass (h_pm) dependence CANCELS.
  2F1 exponent c-a-b = -1  (=-1 -> simple pole in (1-x) -> in (Z-1))
  leading G coefficient (of (1-(1+Z)/2)^-1) = H**2/(16*pi**2)
  d(leading coeff)/d nu = 0  (=0 -> LEADING coincidence singularity is MASS-INDEPENDENT)
   [PASS] massive dS 2-point has a SIMPLE pole at coincidence Z=1 (exponent c-a-b=-1, all masses)
   [PASS] LEADING coincidence coefficient is MASS-INDEPENDENT (Hadamard universality): d/dnu = 0

